      "sample_index": 2,
      "text": "Given expressions for $f$ and $g$:\n$(a+2x-5)=3-4$\n\nWe want to combine like terms, obtaining:\n${x + a - 2x - 5 = 3 - 4}$\n\nSimplifying, we move like terms to the sides with a common factor of $1$:\n${x - 2x + a - 5 = -1}$\n\nCombining like terms, we have:\n${-x + a - 5 = -1}$\n\nTo isolate $a$, subtract $-x$ from both sides and add $5$:\n${a - 5 + 5 = -1 + x + 5}$\n\nThus,\n${a = x + 4}$\n\nSubstituting $a$ back into the equation, we have:\n${x + 2x - 5 = 3 - 4}$\n\nSimplifying further, we are left with:\n${3x - 5 = -1}$\n\nFrom here, we solve for $x$:\n${3x - 5 = -1}$\n\nAdd $5$ to both sides:\n${3x = 4}$\n\nThen divide both sides by $3$:\n${x = \\frac{4}{3}}$\n\nSince a is $x+4$, we ask $x$ to be equal $9$:\n${9 + 4 = \\frac{4}{3}}$\n\nCombining like terms we get:\n${13 = \\frac{4}{3}}$\n\nThe given problem states the left side of the equation equal the right side of the equation, so both sides must be equal to $5$:\n${13 = x + 4 = \\frac{4}{3}}$\n\nTherefore, you would solve it as such:\n${x + 4 = 13 + 4 = 17}$\n\nIn conclusion, the value for $a+b$ is:\n${a+b = 17}$",
