      "sample_index": 3,
      "text": "Since $f(x) = ax + b$, and $g(f(x)) = 3x + 4$, we have \n$g(f(x))=g[af(x)+b]$\n\n$=g[ax+b]=2(ax+b)-5$\n\n$\\implies  g[ax+b]=2ax+2b-5$\n\nBut we know $g(f(x))=3x+4$\n\nTherefore,\n$2ax+2b-5=3x+4$\n\nBut this is given for all $x$, so\n$a=3$ and $2b-5=4$\n\nAnd so\n$b=4.5$\n\nThus, $a+b=3+4.5=\\boxed{7.5}$ Explanation: \n\nGiven the functions \\( f(x) = ax + b \\) and \\( g(x) = 2x - 5 \\), we want to find the constants \\( a \\) and \\( b \\) such that the composition \\( g(f(x)) = 3x + 4 \\) holds for all \\( x \\).\n\nFirst, substitute \\( f(x) \\) into \\( g(x) \\):\n\n\\[ g(f(x)) = g[ax + b] \\]\n\nSince \\( g(x) = 2x - 5 \\), we have:\n\n\\[ g[ax + b] = 2(ax + b) - 5 \\]\n\nExpanding this expression, we get:\n\n\\[ 2(ax + b) - 5 = 2ax + 2b - 5 \\]\n\nGiven that \\( g(f(x)) = 3x + 4 \\), we equate this to the expanded form:\n\n\\[ 2ax + 2b - 5 = 3x + 4 \\]\n\nFor these two expressions to be equal for all \\( x \\), the coefficients of \\( x \\) and the constant terms must be equal on both sides of the equation. Therefore, we can set up the following system of equations:\n\n1. \\( 2a = 3 \\)\n2. \\( 2b - 5 = 4 \\)\n\nSolving the first equation for \\( a \\):\n\n\\[ 2a = 3 \\]\n\\[ a = \\frac{3}{2} \\]\n\nSolving the second equation for \\( b \\):\n\n\\[ 2b - 5 = 4 \\]\n\\[ 2b = 9 \\]\n\\[ b = \\frac{9}{2} \\]\n\nNow, we add \\( a \\) and \\( b \\):\n\n\\[ a + b = \\frac{3}{2} + \\frac{9}{2} = \\frac{12}{2} = 6 \\]\n\nThus, \\( a + b = 6 \\), and the final answer is:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{6}\n\\]",
